{"qid":"Q1132786","names":["Wadai Empire"],"title":"Wadai Empire","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Wadai Empire (also Sultanate of Wadai) was an Islamic state east of Lake Chad in present-day eastern Chad and northeastern Central African Republic, founded in the mid-seventeenth century when Abd al-Karim overthrew the Tunjur rulers and established a Muslim sultanate. Under Sultan Muhammad al-Sharif (r. 1835–1858) Wadai reached its maximum power, raiding as far as the Nile corridor for slaves and defeating the neighboring Bagirmi and Darfur sultanates. French colonial forces defeated Sultan Dud Murra in 1909 and formally annexed Wadai in 1912, incorporating it into French Equatorial Africa.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Wadai's territorial extent is poorly mapped compared to more literate polities; French military surveys from 1900–1912 provide the best cartographic record, but earlier centuries rely on traveller accounts of uncertain precision.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Wadai","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Wadai"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Wadai Empire","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wadai_Empire"},{"label":"John Lavers, 'Kanem and Borno' in J.F.A. Ajayi & M. Crowder (eds.), History of West Africa Vol. 1 (Longman, 1985)","url":"https://archive.org/details/historyofwestaFr0001ajay"}],"britannica":"place/Wadai","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Wadai Empire","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Abd al-Karim ibn Jama","nativeName":"عبد الكريم بن جامع","roman":"ʿAbd al-Karīm ibn Jāmiʿ","start":1635,"end":1655,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Khursan","